On WDBC, Fixed HQNN achieves the highest CV accuracy ($98.24\%$) and CV ROC-AUC ($0.9962$). Adaptive HQNN achieves $96.92\%$ CV accuracy and $0.9923$ CV ROC-AUC, while holdout test ROC-AUC matches the Classical MLP baseline at $0.9907$. On Heart Disease, Classical MLP achieves $83.45\%$ CV accuracy, whereas Adaptive HQNN achieves $76.02\%$ CV accuracy but demonstrates robust generalization on the holdout test set with an F1-score of $85.25\%$ and ROC-AUC of $0.9524$. On Pima Diabetes, Adaptive HQNN achieves $73.94\%$ CV accuracy and $66.67\%$ holdout test F1-score, outperforming the Adaptive Classical Control ($59.83\%$).

\subsection{Quantum Resource Efficiency and Dynamic Routing Distribution}
TABLE~\ref{tab:efficiency} presents the distribution of inference routing allocations and the resulting estimated quantum workload reductions.

\begin{table*}[htbp]
\caption{Inference Routing Allocation and Estimated Quantum Workload Reduction}
\label{tab:efficiency}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Dataset & Classical-only (0 Q) & 1 Quantum Layer & 2 Quantum Layers & Avg Q-Layers / Sample & Workload Reduction (\%) \\
\midrule
WDBC Breast Cancer & 98.0 $\pm$ 0.9\% & 1.5 $\pm$ 1.0\% & 0.4 $\pm$ 0.6\% & \textbf{0.02 $\pm$ 0.01} & \textbf{98.8\% Saved} \\
UCI Heart Disease & 79.8 $\pm$ 7.3\% & 16.1 $\pm$ 8.9\% & 4.1 $\pm$ 4.2\% & \textbf{0.24 $\pm$ 0.08} & \textbf{87.8\% Saved} \\
Pima Indians Diabetes & 71.0 $\pm$ 4.0\% & 17.3 $\pm$ 9.1\% & 11.7 $\pm$ 7.7\% & \textbf{0.41 $\pm$ 0.08} & \textbf{79.6\% Saved} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

On WDBC, $98.0\%$ of patient instances are resolved via Path 0 without invoking quantum simulation, yielding a $98.8\%$ reduction in estimated quantum layer executions relative to Fixed HQNN ($0.02$ vs. $2.00$ layers/sample). On Heart Disease, $79.8\%$ of instances are routed to Path 0, $16.1\%$ to Path 1, and $4.1\%$ to Path 2, achieving an $87.8\%$ workload reduction ($0.24$ layers/sample). On Pima Diabetes, where feature overlap is higher, $71.0\%$ of instances use Path 0, while $29.0\%$ require quantum escalation ($17.3\%$ Path 1, $11.7\%$ Path 2), resulting in a $79.6\%$ estimated reduction ($0.41$ layers/sample).

\subsection{Statistical Hypothesis Testing}
Paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests were performed across matching cross-validation folds ($N=5$ per dataset). For the primary comparison between Adaptive HQNN (M3) and Fixed HQNN (M2), the paired tests yielded:
\begin{itemize}
    \item WDBC: Accuracy $W=0.0, p=0.250$; F1 $W=0.0, p=0.250$; ROC-AUC $W=1.0, p=0.125$.
    \item Heart Disease: Accuracy $W=0.0, p=0.250$; F1 $W=1.0, p=0.250$; ROC-AUC $W=0.0, p=0.0625$.
    \item Pima Diabetes: Accuracy $W=0.0, p=0.0625$; F1 $W=0.0, p=0.0625$; ROC-AUC $W=0.0, p=0.0625$.
\end{itemize}
In all individual benchmark tests, $p \ge 0.05$. Under standard statistical testing protocol, no statistically significant difference was detected in the paired Wilcoxon analysis.

\subsection{Explainable AI and Routing Associations}
Global Kernel SHAP analysis identified the dominant predictive features for each modality: `worst concave points` and `worst perimeter` for WDBC; `thalach` (maximum heart rate) and `oldpeak` (ST depression) for Heart Disease; and `Glucose` and `BMI` for Diabetes.

Surrogate routing analysis indicated that instances allocated to Path 1 and Path 2 exhibited biomarker distributions near diagnostic threshold boundaries. In Diabetes, Path 2 instances displayed greater dispersion in plasma glucose and BMI compared to high-confidence Path 0 instances. In Heart Disease, cases routed to quantum layers were associated with intermediate ST depression values ($1.0 \le \text{oldpeak} \le 2.5$). These observations represent empirical behavioral associations of the routing mechanism rather than causal biological relationships.

\section{Discussion}
The experimental results demonstrate a clear, measurable trade-off between predictive performance and estimated inference-time quantum workload. 

First, the magnitude of quantum layer reduction correlates with intrinsic dataset separability. On WDBC, where morphological features offer high linear and non-linear separability, the preliminary classical encoder achieves high confidence for $98\%$ of instances, rendering quantum processing largely redundant. Conversely, in Pima Diabetes and Heart Disease, where physiological distributions overlap substantially, the adaptive router escalates $20\%$ to $29\%$ of instances to quantum variational layers.

Second, the reduction in estimated inference workload is accompanied by a slight decrease in cross-validation F1-score relative to Fixed HQNN ($-1.73\%$ on WDBC, $-3.84\%$ on Heart, $-4.09\%$ on Diabetes). This illustrates the operational trade-off of adaptive execution: routing borderline instances through shallower pathways can introduce minor decision boundary shifts. However, generalization on the holdout test set remained competitive, with Adaptive HQNN achieving an F1-score of $85.25\%$ on Heart Disease (identical to Fixed HQNN) and $66.67\%$ on Diabetes (exceeding Fixed HQNN's $65.04\%$).

Third, comparisons with the Adaptive Classical Control (M4) reveal that Adaptive HQNN maintains higher cross-validation F1-scores across all three benchmarks ($95.88\%$ vs. $94.48\%$ on WDBC, $73.49\%$ vs. $72.90\%$ on Heart, and $66.27\%$ vs. $65.04\%$ on Diabetes), indicating that variational quantum layers provide expressive latent transformations when selectively engaged.

\section{Limitations and Threats to Validity}
Several limitations must be explicitly acknowledged:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dataset Scale}: Benchmark datasets represent relatively small cohorts ($N=303$ to $768$). While standard in literature, small sample sizes limit statistical power and generalizability.
    \item \textbf{Simulation vs. Hardware}: Quantum circuits were evaluated using state-vector numerical simulation. Physical NISQ processors exhibit device-specific gate noise, readout crosstalk, and thermal decoherence.
    \item \textbf{Workload Metric}: Quantum workload is quantified as estimated layer executions per sample. This does not directly measure cryogenic power, physical execution time, or compilation overheads.
    \item \textbf{No Quantum Advantage}: This study does not demonstrate quantum advantage or supremacy over classical algorithms, as classical MLPs achieve competitive metrics.
    \item \textbf{XAI Scope}: SHAP attributions reflect model decision associations and do not imply causal clinical pathology.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
This paper introduced Adaptive HQNN, a hybrid quantum-classical architecture featuring uncertainty-guided dynamic quantum layer selection for medical tabular classification. Across three benchmark datasets, the proposed method achieved estimated inference-time quantum layer reductions of 79.6\% to 98.8\% compared to fixed-depth quantum models, with paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests detecting no statistically significant difference in ROC-AUC ($p \ge 0.05$).

Future work will investigate deploying Adaptive HQNN on physical superconducting and trapped-ion quantum processors, evaluating multi-class diagnostic extensions, and incorporating Bayesian uncertainty quantification within the dynamic routing policy.

